Immediate implementation of a "debridement plus protection" strategy is essential:  
① Use a sharp instrument to cut along the boundary of the lesion, removing 0.5 cm of healthy tissue beyond the affected area; thoroughly scrape away all discolored bark and superficial wood until fresh green cambium is exposed;  
② Immediately apply Bordeaux paste (1:1:10) or Mingbawei solution diluted 100-fold to cover the entire wound surface and extend 1 cm into adjacent healthy bark;  
③ Simultaneously clear weeds around the tree base, eliminate standing water, and strengthen water and nutrient management to enhance tree vigor.  
Note: Avoid extensive bark removal during periods of high summer temperatures to prevent exacerbation of sunscald. If the lesion encircles more than one-third of the trunk’s circumference, consider bridge grafting or preparing for crown replacement. This stage represents a critical early intervention window—prompt treatment can effectively halt pathogen progression toward the pith.

It should be addressed as soon as possible. Key operational steps:  
① Using a sharp knife, make a circular incision 1–2 cm beyond the boundary between diseased and healthy tissue, then vertically remove all discolored bark layers until healthy light-yellow wood is fully exposed (avoid damaging the cambium);  
② Immediately disinfect the exposed surface with 75% alcohol or flame sterilization after scraping;  
③ Apply a protective agent: recommend Bordeaux paste (1:1:10) or 100-fold dilution of Methyl Thiophanate, ensuring coverage extends at least 2 cm beyond the scraped area;  
④ Avoid exposure to rain for at least 3 days post-treatment; in summer, perform operations in late afternoon to prevent sunscald.  
Note: If numerous small white spots are actively producing spores (visible as powdery substance), apply mancozeb spray to seal spores prior to scraping to prevent further spread.

Preventive spraying has limited efficacy, as pathogens primarily invade through wounds, and existing chemical agents struggle to establish protective barriers deep within bark. More effective strategies focus on breaking the disease transmission cycle:  
① During spring and autumn (November–April), spray all healthy branches and trunks in the orchard twice with a copper-based fungicide (e.g., copper hydroxide diluted 800-fold), with special emphasis on the main trunk crotch area and branches facing southwest;  
② Apply wound protectants immediately within 2 hours after pruning (e.g., Trichoderma bio-agent or a mixture of tung oil and sulfur powder at a 10:1 ratio);  
③ Remove and burn fallen twigs and leaves away from the orchard—particularly avoid pruning during summer, when airborne spore concentrations of pathogens such as Neoscytalidium are highest. Chemical control should be reserved for localized treatment of already infected trees and is not recommended for whole-tree prophylactic spraying.

Currently, the disease is in its early to middle stage (visible spots present but leaf margins remain intact and no large-scale defoliation has occurred). A dual strategy of "removal + prevention" should be adopted:  
① Immediately remove severely yellowed, spotted old leaves, take them out of the field, and destroy them to reduce pathogen load;  
② Apply protective fungicides—such as mancozeb or copper-based products (e.g., Bordeaux mixture)—to remaining healthy foliage and new shoots, with particular attention to the leaf surfaces, especially the undersides of older mid-to-lower leaves. This is critical because imaging data indicate that lesions typically originate in the vein regions, where the underside often serves as a hotspot for spore germination. Chemical control should be applied before rain or after morning dew has dried to prevent wash-off of the spray; if continuous rainfall has occurred within the past seven days, it is recommended to reapply the treatment 10–14 days after the initial application. Avoid relying solely on leaf pruning without spraying, as residual inoculum will continue to reinfect new growth.

It is still timely—this falls within the early preventive window. Immediate implementation of a combined strategy—“localized removal + canopy spraying”—is recommended:  
① Remove and deeply bury or incinerate infected fruits to prevent sporulation and spread;  
② Spray the entire orchard with copper-based fungicides (e.g., copper hydroxide or Bordeaux mixture), with particular emphasis on the mid-to-lower canopy fruits and leaf undersides, as Alternaria conidia are easily wind-dispersed and require uniform, thorough coverage;  
③ If rainfall is forecasted in the near term, consider adding phosphoramidate fungicides (e.g., potassium phosphite) to enhance systemic resistance.  
Note: Avoid application during high temperatures and strong sunlight to prevent phytotoxicity; refrain from using formulations with excessive manganese or zinc content within 30 days prior to harvest. Prefer organic-certified copper-zinc-lime formulations (e.g., 3-2-6-100), applying 4 ounces per 100 gallons of water with an adjuvant (e.g., spreader-sticker) to improve adherence and efficacy.

Priority should be given to agricultural management measures to delay disease progression:  
① Immediately remove infected fruits and the dense foliage within 30 cm below them to improve ventilation and light penetration, thereby reducing surface moisture on fruits;  
② Control irrigation frequency, avoid evening watering, ensure proper drainage in the orchard, and maintain relative humidity below 85% to suppress spore germination;  
③ Apply additional potassium fertilizer (e.g., potassium sulfate) and calcium fertilizer to strengthen the cuticular wax layer and cell wall integrity of the fruit, enhancing resistance to pathogen invasion.  
If lesions expand within 7 days or new fruits exhibit similar symptoms, chemical control must be initiated immediately. Note: Under high summer temperatures, Alternaria spp. reproduce rapidly; cultural practices alone are only effective for sporadic infections (≤3 fruits per tree). For infections exceeding 5 fruits per tree, copper-based fungicides combined with phospholipid-based formulations should be applied promptly.

Three-step isolation and prevention measures must be implemented immediately:  
① Remove diseased leaves and fallen dead leaves, then centrally incinerate or bury them to eliminate spore sources;  
② Avoid pruning, harvesting, and other operations during humid weather to minimize human-mediated spread;  
③ Establish physical barriers around infected trees (e.g., laying agricultural film or applying lime powder) to block splash dispersal by rain.  
Meanwhile, enhance tree management: apply calcium and magnesium fertilizers to increase the thickness of the cuticular wax layer, thereby reducing pathogen adhesion success; use drip irrigation instead of overhead spraying during watering, ensuring leaf surfaces remain dry for more than 8 hours per day to suppress spore germination. Chemical control should serve only as supplementary support and never replace sanitation practices or moisture management.

No immediate pesticide application is required. First, confirm the species: the individual in the image is relatively large with distinct spots, strongly suggesting it is a beneficial insect (such as a ladybug), not Lewis mite. The latter is minute (requiring a hand lens for observation), lacks spots, and resides exclusively on the leaf undersides. If the pest is indeed a mite, and only one individual is present without visible leaf symptoms, this indicates an early detection stage; non-chemical control measures should be prioritized:  
① Examine the undersides of five or more leaves (both young and old) on the same plant to detect tiny red dots, webbing, or chlorotic spots;  
② Increase irrigation during hot, dry periods to raise humidity and suppress mite reproduction;  
③ Protect natural enemies (e.g., Amblyseius obtusus) by avoiding broad-spectrum pesticides that could inadvertently kill them.  
Pesticide application—using low-toxicity options such as spiromesifen—should only be considered when mite density exceeds 2 individuals per field of view (10× magnification) on the leaf underside and chlorotic symptoms are evident.

This is the egg sac of the cottony cushion scale insect, not a fungus. Key distinguishing features are:  
① The egg sac is elongated-oval in shape, with longitudinal wrinkles, soft and fluffy like cotton, firmly attached to branches or leaf axils and not easily dislodged;  
② It is invariably connected below to a brownish-gray, flat-bodied adult female insect;  
③ Fungal lesions (such as sooty mold) appear as adherent, gray-black powdery layers on leaf surfaces, lacking a three-dimensional, fibrous structure, and are never associated with fixed insects.  
On-site, use a hand lens for observation: if faintly visible orange-red tiny eggs (approximately 0.3 mm in size) are seen within the fluff, the diagnosis is confirmed as a cottony cushion scale egg sac.

Currently in the early localized infestation stage (3–5 insects per fruit), non-chemical measures are prioritized:  
① Gently brush aphids and wax residues off with a soft brush dipped in soapy water to physically remove adults and nymphs;  
② Inspect the undersides of adjacent leaves and branch crotches; if additional nymphs are found (especially reddish-brown spots), apply either 22% sulfoxaflor suspension concentrate diluted 3,000-fold or 0.5% matrine aqueous solution diluted 600-fold, with thorough spraying directed at leaf undersides and tender shoots;  
③ Avoid using broad-spectrum pesticides such as imidacloprid and beta-cyfluthrin to prevent accidental mortality of natural enemies (e.g., Australian ladybugs). If no new infestations appear and black spots decrease within 7 days, chemical control may be postponed; otherwise, during peak nymph development periods (typically March–April in spring and September–October in autumn), apply 50% thiamethoxam water-dispersible granules at a rate of 240–480 g a.i./hm² via targeted spraying.

Currently, the pest is in its early nymphal stage with concealed feeding behavior. We recommend prioritizing biological control combined with physical monitoring:  
① Immediately inspect the undersides of leaves, bases of tender shoots, and branch crotches for red-colored nymphs or white waxy secretions;  
② If live individuals are detected, release *Vedalia* (actually *Rodolia cardinalis*, a ladybird beetle) as a natural enemy—release 20–25 adult or larval individuals per 10 acres, which can effectively suppress populations within 4–6 weeks;  
③ Chemical control should only be applied when natural enemies are absent and population density increases: use 50% thiamethoxam water-dispersible granules at 240–960 g a.i./hm², focusing applications on the lower portions of fruits and leaf undersides, avoiding flowering periods and high-temperature times. Broad-spectrum insecticides (e.g., imidacloprid, beta-cypermethrin) must be prohibited to prevent harm to beneficial organisms.

A large number of male insects appearing indicates a high population base of females and that the peak egg-laying period has passed, signaling the imminent onset of a nymph hatching surge—this is a critical early-warning phase. Immediate three-step intervention is required:  
① Hang yellow sticky traps throughout the orchard (20–30 traps per acre) to reduce male mating success;  
② Apply targeted sprays of 1.2%% matrine aqueous solution diluted 800-fold or 25%% thiamethoxam diluted 7,500-fold, focusing on nymph aggregation sites such as new shoots, leaf axils, and fruit stems, while avoiding spraying during periods of high activity of natural enemies;  
③ Simultaneously remove weeds and prune overly dense branches to improve air circulation and reduce shelter availability.  
Avoid broad-spectrum insecticide applications across the entire orchard, as this may eliminate beneficial natural enemies such as the Taiwan lady beetle and Encarsia aphidius, potentially leading to pest outbreaks later in the season.

Chemical control is not currently necessary. The current population density falls within the monitoring or early outbreak phase (approximately 10–30 individuals per leaf), and healthy orchards in spring can tolerate higher densities. Non-chemical measures should be prioritized:  
① Inspect the undersides of leaves and adjacent new shoots to confirm the presence of predatory mites (e.g., Neoseiulus barkeri, slightly larger, pear-shaped, semi-transparent);  
② If irrigation is insufficient, promptly provide supplemental water to alleviate tree stress;  
③ Consider applying a mineral oil emulsion (e.g., 415 narrow-range oil at 1.2% concentration) via localized spraying, focusing on leaf undersides and new growth—this effectively controls eggs and young mites while minimizing harm to natural enemies. Selective miticides such as etoxazole or flumetramid should only be considered if monitoring shows sustained densities exceeding 8 female mites per leaf for two consecutive weeks and natural enemy populations are low.

Monitoring and early intervention should be initiated immediately:  
① Install one phototactic insect light trap every 150 meters to attract and kill adult insects;  
② Set up specialized sex pheromone traps for the armyworm (one trap per 150 m²) in the field, focusing on capturing male moths to reduce mating rates;  
③ Conduct simultaneous surveys of the undersides of upper leaves. If egg masses or first- to second-instar larvae are detected, apply a 1.8% abamectin emulsifiable concentrate diluted at 1,500 times, with emphasis on spraying the heart leaves and inner leaf heads. The best application timing is in the morning or evening under clear weather conditions.
